const intro={"part":"vuelta","c":2,"slug":"vuelta-canto-2","title":"Fierro and Cruz Arrive Among Indigenous Communities","summary":"Fierro recalls the sorrow of leaving his home and family, then tells how he and Cruz entered the country beyond the frontier and reached an Indigenous settlement. They arrive during a gathering about a possible raid and are suspected of being spies. Their horses are taken and they are threatened. A man who interprets for the group eventually informs them that a leader has spared their lives so they may be held for a possible prisoner exchange. Fierro then describes armed gatherings, surveillance and questioning. The descriptions of Indigenous people are hostile and sweeping: they are the speaker's perspective, not a balanced account of Indigenous societies.","people":"Martín Fierro and Sergeant Cruz; Indigenous leaders and mounted members of the settlement as Fierro describes them; an interpreter (lenguaraz) who explains the leader's decision.","themes":"Exile and family loss; arrival in unfamiliar territory; captivity and prisoner exchange; fear and suspicion; the narrator's anti-Indigenous prejudices; separating poetic testimony from history.","words":"toldería or toldos (Indigenous camp or settlement); parlamento (council or meeting); lenguaraz (interpreter); cacique (leader); malón (raid, a historically situated term); huincá (Indigenous term used here for outsiders or non-Indigenous people).","questions":["Why does Fierro continue recalling his family before describing the settlement?","What do the residents fear when Fierro and Cruz arrive, according to the narration?","What explanation does the interpreter give for keeping the two men alive?","Which statements describe particular events and which unfairly generalize about entire Indigenous peoples?","What external historical sources would help test the narrator's claims?"],"nums":[429,437,438],"analysis":"The text explicitly reports suspicion, a threatened killing, a decision to 